User stories: Order management system

Basis: I wrote these from Part 1 only (REQ-F-01 to REQ-F-32 and REQ-N-01 to REQ-N-08). Part 2 is marked “for evaluators only”, so I didn’t use it. Each acceptance criterion is tagged with the requirement it comes from.

Roles:

User: any authenticated user of the system (REQ-N-05). The requirements name no other operational roles.
Accountant: a user holding the accountant role (REQ-N-06).
Customer: the person whose data is stored (REQ-F-01, REQ-N-07).
Business: the organisation operating the system. I use this role only in quality stories where the requirements name no beneficiary.
Epic A: Customer and product data
US-01: Register a customer

As a user, I want to record a customer’s name, address and phone number, and optionally a bank account, so that orders can be submitted for that customer.

Acceptance criteria

A customer is stored with a name, an address and a phone number. (REQ-F-01)
A customer can be stored with a bank account or without one. (REQ-F-02)
US-02: Register a product

As a user, I want to record a product’s name, description, price and image, so that the product can be ordered.

Acceptance criteria

A product is stored with a name, a description, a price and an image. (REQ-F-03)
Epic B: Order submission
US-03: Submit an order

As a user, I want to submit an order for a customer with one or more order items and a payment method, so that the customer’s order is registered.

Acceptance criteria

An order submission contains a customer, one or more order items and a payment method. (REQ-F-04)
A valid submission is registered as an order with an order date and the status pending. (REQ-F-09)
Each order item is recorded with a quantity, a unit price and a reference to exactly one product. (REQ-F-10)
The system determines the total amount of the order. (REQ-F-08)
The order is associated with exactly one customer, the one it was submitted for. (REQ-F-11)
A customer may have any number of orders, including none. A customer who already has orders can have more registered. (REQ-F-11)
US-04: Reject invalid order submissions

As a user, I want invalid order submissions to be rejected, so that only valid orders are registered.

Acceptance criteria

If the customer is not known to the system, the submission is rejected and no order is registered. (REQ-F-05)
If the submission contains no order item, it is rejected and no order is registered. (REQ-F-06)
If any order item refers to a product that does not exist, the submission is rejected and no order is registered, even when the other items are valid. (REQ-F-07)
Epic C: Order fulfilment
US-05: Accept an order

As a user, I want to set an order’s status to accepted, so that the order can move forward.

Acceptance criteria

An order with status pending can be set to accepted. (REQ-F-12)
An attempt to set an order to accepted from any other status is rejected, and the status stays the same. (REQ-F-30, see US-17)
US-06: Ship an order

As a user, I want to set an order’s status to shipped, so that dispatch of the order is recorded.

Acceptance criteria

An order with status paid can be set to shipped. (REQ-F-24)
An attempt to set an order to shipped from any other status is rejected, and the status stays the same. (REQ-F-30)
US-07: Close an order

As a user, I want to set an order’s status to closed, so that the order is marked as finished.

Acceptance criteria

An order with status shipped can be set to closed. (REQ-F-25)
An attempt to set an order to closed from any other status is rejected, and the status stays the same. (REQ-F-30)
Epic D: Invoicing
US-08: Raise an invoice

As a user, I want to raise an invoice for an order, so that the order can be billed.

Acceptance criteria

An invoice is raised for an order with a date, an amount, a payment method and the status pending. (REQ-F-13)
If the order already has an invoice, the attempt to raise another one is rejected. (REQ-F-14)
An order may have no invoice. (REQ-F-14: “at most one”)
US-09: Issue an invoice

As a user, I want to set an invoice’s status to issued, so that the invoice is formally issued.

Acceptance criteria

An invoice with status pending can be set to issued. (REQ-F-15)
An attempt to issue an invoice that has already been issued is rejected, and the status stays the same. (REQ-F-16)
An attempt to set a paid invoice to issued is rejected. (REQ-F-31)
Epic E: Payments
US-10: Submit a payment

As a user, I want to submit a payment against an invoice, so that it can be confirmed or rejected.

Acceptance criteria

A payment is submitted against an invoice with a date, an amount and a payment method. (REQ-F-17)
A submitted payment is recorded with the status pending, awaiting confirmation. (REQ-F-19)
Any number of payments can be submitted against the same invoice. (REQ-F-18)
US-11: Confirm a payment

As an accountant, I want to confirm a pending payment, so that its invoice and order are recorded as paid.

Acceptance criteria

A payment with status pending can be confirmed, which sets its status to completed. (REQ-F-20)
When the payment is confirmed, the status of its invoice is set to paid. (REQ-F-22)
After the invoice has been set to paid, the status of its order is set to paid. (REQ-F-23)
A payment that is not pending cannot be confirmed. (REQ-F-31)
Only a user with the accountant role can confirm a payment. (REQ-N-06, see US-25)
US-12: Reject a payment

As an accountant, I want to reject a pending payment, so that it is recorded as failed without affecting its invoice or order.

Acceptance criteria

A payment with status pending can be rejected, which sets its status to failed. (REQ-F-21)
Rejecting a payment does not change the status of its invoice or its order. (REQ-F-21)
A payment that is not pending cannot be rejected. (REQ-F-31)
Only a user with the accountant role can reject a payment. (REQ-N-06, see US-25)
Epic F: Business and data rules
US-13: Mandatory attributes

As a user, I want every attribute to be required except a customer’s bank account, so that no record is stored incomplete.

Acceptance criteria

A record missing any of these attributes is rejected (REQ-F-26):
Customer: name, address, phone number
Product: name, description, price, image
Order: order date, status, total amount, payment method
Order item: quantity, unit price, product reference
Invoice: date, amount, payment method, status
Payment: date, amount, payment method, status
A customer’s bank account is the only optional attribute. (REQ-F-26, REQ-F-02)
US-14: Monetary values as decimals

As a user, I want all monetary values held as decimal numbers, so that amounts are represented exactly.

Acceptance criteria

Product price, order item unit price, order total amount, invoice amount and payment amount are all decimal numbers. (REQ-F-27)
US-15: Allowed status values

As a user, I want each status limited to a defined set of values, so that no record can hold an undefined status.

Acceptance criteria

Order status is one of: pending, accepted, paid, shipped, closed. (REQ-F-28)
Invoice status is one of: pending, issued, paid. (REQ-F-28)
Payment status is one of: pending, completed, failed. (REQ-F-28)
Any other status value is rejected. (REQ-F-28)
US-16: Allowed payment methods

As a user, I want the payment method limited to bank, card or cash, so that only supported methods are recorded.

Acceptance criteria

Wherever a payment method is recorded (order, invoice, payment), it is one of: bank, card, cash. (REQ-F-29)
Any other value is rejected. (REQ-F-29)
US-17: Order status sequence

As a user, I want an order’s status to change only in the defined sequence, so that no step of the order lifecycle is skipped or reversed.

Acceptance criteria

The only allowed changes are pending → accepted → paid → shipped → closed. (REQ-F-30)
Any other change is rejected and the status stays the same. This includes skipping a step, going backwards, and any change from closed. (REQ-F-30)
US-18: Invoice and payment status sequences

As a user, I want invoice and payment statuses to change only along their defined paths, so that the billing and payment lifecycle stays consistent.

Acceptance criteria

The only allowed invoice status changes are pending → issued → paid. (REQ-F-31)
The only allowed payment status changes are pending → completed and pending → failed. (REQ-F-31)
A payment that is completed or failed cannot change status again. (REQ-F-31)
Any other change is rejected and the status stays the same. (REQ-F-31)
US-19: Order paid only after its invoice is paid

As a user, I want an order to be set to paid only once its invoice is paid, so that an order is never marked paid without payment.

Acceptance criteria

An attempt to set an order to paid is rejected unless its invoice has status paid. This includes an order that has no invoice. (REQ-F-32)
Epic G: Quality attributes
US-20: Order submission response time

As a user, I want order submission to respond quickly, so that I can register orders without delay.

Acceptance criteria

Under normal operating conditions, at least 95% of submissions with up to 50 order items are registered and answered within 2 seconds. (REQ-N-01)
US-21: Order submission capacity

As a user, I want the system to handle many users and submissions at once, so that response times hold at peak use.

Acceptance criteria

Under normal operating conditions, the system supports at least 200 concurrent users and at least 20 order submissions per second. (REQ-N-02)
At that load, the response time of US-20 is still met. (REQ-N-02)
US-22: Monthly availability

As a user, I want the system to be available nearly all the time, so that I can rely on it for daily work.

Acceptance criteria

The system is available at least 99.5% of each calendar month. (REQ-N-03)
Only scheduled maintenance announced at least 24 hours in advance is excluded from the calculation. (REQ-N-03)
US-23: Recovery from server failure

As a user, I want the system to recover quickly from a server failure without losing data, so that recorded orders, invoices and payments are never lost.

Acceptance criteria

After a server failure, the system accepts requests again within 15 minutes. (REQ-N-04)
No order, invoice or payment recorded before the failure is lost. (REQ-N-04)
US-24: Authentication

As a customer, I want only authenticated users to access my data, so that my information is not exposed to unknown parties.

Acceptance criteria

Every user is authenticated before getting access to any customer, order, invoice or payment data. (REQ-N-05)
100% of unauthenticated requests are rejected. (REQ-N-05)
US-25: Accountant-only payment decisions

As an accountant, I want only users with the accountant role to be able to confirm or reject payments, so that payment decisions stay under accounting control.

Acceptance criteria

A user with the accountant role can confirm or reject a payment. (REQ-N-06)
The same request from any other user is rejected. (REQ-N-06)
US-26: Data encryption

As a customer, I want my personal data and bank account number encrypted, so that they are kept confidential.

Acceptance criteria

Customer personal data and bank account numbers are sent only over TLS 1.2 or higher. (REQ-N-07)
Customer personal data and bank account numbers are encrypted at rest. (REQ-N-07)
US-27: Audit trail of status changes

As the business, I want every status change to be recorded with who made it and when, so that changes can be traced afterwards.

Acceptance criteria

For every status change of an order, an invoice or a payment, the system records the user and the time. (REQ-N-08)
These records are kept for at least 12 months. (REQ-N-08)
